WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.062
The self-rescuer you carry at our sites is the

00:07.062 --> 00:11.750
M S A W sixty-five. It is a filter self-rescuer.

00:11.750 --> 00:14.237
Inside is a catalyst called hopcalite.

00:14.237 --> 00:16.888
When you breathe through it, it turns toxic carbon

00:16.888 --> 00:20.088
monoxide into carbon dioxide, which is far less

00:20.088 --> 00:23.175
dangerous to you. It also has dust filters,

00:23.175 --> 00:25.625
and a drying agent that protects the catalyst from

00:25.625 --> 00:26.550
moisture.

00:27.684 --> 00:31.047
It's made for one thing: escaping from carbon monoxide

00:31.047 --> 00:33.759
after an underground fire or explosion.

00:33.759 --> 00:35.822
It is a one-time escape device.

00:36.955 --> 00:40.342
Here's the point to hold onto. The W65 does not

00:40.342 --> 00:43.005
make oxygen and does not store oxygen.

00:43.005 --> 00:45.230
It cleans carbon monoxide out of the air you're

00:45.230 --> 00:47.842
breathing, and nothing else. It works with the

00:47.842 --> 00:50.617
oxygen that's already in the air around you.

00:53.944 --> 00:55.944
That means it can't protect you in air that is

00:55.944 --> 00:59.007
low on oxygen. The manufacturer's manual says it's

00:59.007 --> 01:01.582
not for use in atmospheres containing less than

01:01.582 --> 01:04.119
nineteen and a half percent oxygen.

01:04.119 --> 01:06.507
In air like that, a filter can't help,

01:06.507 --> 01:09.344
because there's not enough oxygen to filter into.

01:10.474 --> 01:13.536
It's also approved only for carbon monoxide.

01:13.536 --> 01:16.074
The manual says it's not for use in atmospheres

01:16.074 --> 01:18.886
containing other toxic gases and vapors.

01:18.886 --> 01:21.324
It's an escape tool for the carbon monoxide that

01:21.324 --> 01:24.674
comes from a fire or an explosion. It's not a respirator

01:24.674 --> 01:27.199
for working in bad air, and you never use it to

01:27.199 --> 01:29.124
explore or check an area.

01:30.256 --> 01:33.456
The W65 gives you time. It doesn't take you out

01:33.456 --> 01:37.044
by itself. Time only helps if you know your escapeways,

01:37.044 --> 01:39.756
follow the mine's emergency plan, and keep moving

01:39.756 --> 01:43.294
toward fresh air. Your site's plans always control.

01:46.622 --> 01:50.097
When the W65 is working on carbon monoxide,

01:50.097 --> 01:52.384
the air you breathe through it will be hot and

01:52.384 --> 01:56.159
dry. The more carbon monoxide, the hotter it gets.

01:56.159 --> 01:59.109
That's the catalyst doing its job. The manual says

01:59.109 --> 02:01.772
the heat itself is a sign that carbon monoxide

02:01.772 --> 02:02.734
is present.

02:03.848 --> 02:07.048
So hot and dry is normal. It's uncomfortable,

02:07.048 --> 02:09.636
but it is not a reason to take the mouthpiece out.

02:10.754 --> 02:13.279
Don't open your lips to sneak a cooler breath.

02:13.279 --> 02:15.517
The manual warns that doing that can give you an

02:15.517 --> 02:19.179
overexposure to carbon monoxide. It says a concentration

02:19.179 --> 02:22.504
of about five-tenths of one percent can cause collapse,

02:22.504 --> 02:25.642
unconsciousness, and death within a few minutes.

02:25.642 --> 02:28.004
It's far better to be alive with a hot,

02:28.004 --> 02:31.792
even blistered, mouth than overcome by carbon monoxide.

02:32.924 --> 02:35.086
Breathe through it the whole way, keep your nose

02:35.086 --> 02:38.036
clip on, and stay calm. Breathing through the unit

02:38.036 --> 02:40.924
is harder than normal, so avoid exerting yourself

02:40.924 --> 02:43.611
more than you have to, and rest for short periods

02:43.611 --> 02:44.561
if you can.

02:45.698 --> 02:48.023
The manual is just as clear about when to put it

02:48.023 --> 02:51.548
on: at the first sign of a fire or an explosion,

02:51.548 --> 02:54.423
even if you don't see smoke. Carbon monoxide has

02:54.423 --> 02:57.373
no color and no smell, and it can be ahead of the

02:57.373 --> 03:00.436
smoke. Waiting to see smoke can be fatal.

03:01.568 --> 03:04.030
Try this. You're walking out in smoke.

03:04.030 --> 03:07.818
Your W65 is getting hot, and your mouth feels dry.

03:07.818 --> 03:10.368
What do you do? You keep it in. You keep your lips

03:10.368 --> 03:13.243
sealed and the nose clip on, and you keep moving

03:13.243 --> 03:16.043
toward fresh air. The heat tells you the device

03:16.043 --> 03:18.093
is working on carbon monoxide.

03:19.226 --> 03:21.038
And when does it come out? When you've reached

03:21.038 --> 03:24.726
fresh air, not before. Discomfort isn't a reason.

03:24.726 --> 03:26.576
If you're not sure the air is safe,

03:26.576 --> 03:29.276
leave it in, and follow your emergency plan and

03:29.276 --> 03:31.851
whoever is directing the evacuation.

03:35.172 --> 03:38.334
Before you go underground, look at your W65 for

03:38.334 --> 03:40.935
damage. If the container is dented,

03:40.935 --> 03:44.472
or the seal is broken, report it and get it replaced.

03:44.472 --> 03:46.897
Never open a service unit for practice.

03:46.897 --> 03:49.722
Hands-on practice is done on the approved training

03:49.722 --> 03:53.459
unit with your instructor. Once a W65 is opened

03:53.459 --> 03:56.885
or used, it's done, and it has to be replaced.

03:58.014 --> 04:00.727
Check it before you go in, because the manual points

04:00.727 --> 04:03.452
out that a dented container might keep the device

04:03.452 --> 04:06.114
from coming out easily. That's a reason to report

04:06.114 --> 04:08.952
it and swap it before your shift, not something

04:08.952 --> 04:11.514
to find out during an emergency. This is why we

04:11.514 --> 04:13.264
practice on the training unit.

04:14.389 --> 04:17.064
Some older films in this module show other kinds

04:17.064 --> 04:20.551
of escape units, including ones that supply oxygen.

04:20.551 --> 04:23.676
Those don't work like the W65, and our sites don't

04:23.676 --> 04:26.214
issue them. Don't use what a film shows to guess

04:26.214 --> 04:29.564
what your W65 can do. Use what you learned here.

04:30.690 --> 04:34.040
So remember the W65 in five points.

04:34.040 --> 04:37.890
It filters carbon monoxide, and only carbon monoxide.

04:37.890 --> 04:41.015
It supplies no oxygen. It's not for low oxygen

04:41.015 --> 04:44.303
or other gases. Hot, dry air is normal.

04:44.303 --> 04:46.728
And you keep it in until you reach fresh air.

04:47.844 --> 04:50.819
Your site's plans always override this video.
